From 2f000974fd5767b3460343405c16fc2b1b5266a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Volodymyr Kravchenko <volandevo@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 03:31:30 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] fix(host/colour): apply the colour override to the
 handshake too, not just the VUI
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A session describes its colour twice — in the bitstream VUI, and in the
punktfunk/1 `ColorInfo` the handshake carries — and clients read one or the
other. The previous commit moved only the VUI, which is invisible to a client
that configures itself from the handshake.

The webOS/LG client is exactly that client: it hands `ColorInfo.matrix` straight
to `NDL_DirectVideoSetHDRInfo` and never parses the VUI, so on an LG panel the
override changed nothing on the glass while the log showed it applied. The two
fields describe the same conversion, so they now resolve the same knob: the
name→CICP parsing moved to `pf-host-config` (a crate both the encoder and the
handshake already depend on) and each side only translates.

`handshake.rs` had the premise written down — "BT.2020 PQ, what the NVENC HEVC
VUI emits from a 10-bit capture format" — which is where the shared mistake
came from: the handshake was made to mirror the VUI, and the VUI was assumed
rather than measured.

Measured, on an RTX 5090 (identical at 720p/1080p/4K, and identical whether or
not the stream declares BT.2020, so the internal CSC does not read these
fields): feeding NVENC packed RGB and decoding the result back lands on BT.601
limited to within 0.13 code values, against 24-61 for every other candidate.
So a 10-bit session declares BT.2020 NCL and ships BT.601.

Still no guessed default: unset, both sides are byte-for-byte unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
